    "created_at": "2016-11-07T08:50:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-07T08:51:23Z",
    "body": "The `div` wrapping of `dt/dd` wasn't for semantic reasons either, but just for practical purposes. As the OP of #1937 already stated (emphasis mine):\n\n> Explicitly grouping dt and dd together has been proposed so often that it’s featured in the Rationale and has been in the FAQ since 2008, getting further explained in 2010.\n> \n> >    This is a styling problem and should be fixed in CSS. There's no reason to add a grouping element to HTML, **as the semantics are already unambiguous.**\n> \n> There will be no CSS pseudo-element ::wrap in the forseeable future, see w3c/csswg-drafts#588. I believe it’s therefore worth to reevaluate the use cases and review the FAQ entry.\n\nI think a similar argument can be made for `li`. For example, grouping `li`s  can make it significantly easier to display a list in multiple columns without having to split it up in several individual lists (css columns don't always cut it, since AFAIK they don't allow you to target individual columns). \n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
